  "provider_query": "RFC 791 text for the Internet Protocol Version field",
  "answer": "**RFC 791 (Internet Protocol, J. Postel, September 1981) — Version field**\n\n1. **Header definition (Section 3.1)**  \n   `Version: 4 bits`  \n   “The Version field indicates the format of the internet header. This document describes version 4.”  \n   Source: [RFC 791 plain text](https://www.rfc-***/rfc/rfc791.txt) (also in the header diagram as the first 4 bits).\n\n2. **Glossary**  \n   “Version — The Version field indicates the format of the internet header.”  \n   Source: same RFC 791 text (glossary).\n\n3. **Official RFC page**  \n   RFC 791 is an Internet Standard (part of STD 5); obsoletes RFC 760; updated by later RFCs (e.g., 1349, 2474, 6864).  \n   Source: [rfc-***/info/rfc791](https://www.rfc-***/info/rfc791).\n\n4. **IANA IP Version Numbers**  \n   Notes a 4-bit field that identifies the version of the internetwork protocol (citing RFC 791 and RFC 8200). Decimal 4 is assigned to IP (Internet Protocol) per RFC 791.  \n   Source: [IANA version-numbers](https://www.iana.org/assignments/version-numbers/version-numbers.xhtml).\n\n5. **Header layout context**  \n   The first word of the datagram header is shown as `|Version| IHL |Type of Service| Total Length|`, with Version occupying the high-order 4 bits.  \n   Source: RFC 791 Section 3.1 diagram (same official text).\n\n6. **STD 5 compilation**  \n   The same Version wording appears in the STD 5 text that incorporates RFC 791.  \n   Source: [rfc-***/std/std5.txt](https://www.rfc-***/std/std5.txt).\n\n7. **Related registry note**  \n   IANA: the field identifies the version of the internetwork general protocol and is 4 bits; version 4 is Internet Protocol.  \n   Source: [IANA version-numbers.txt](https://www.iana.org/assignments/version-numbers/version-numbers.txt).\n\n8. **Document status**  \n   Title: Internet Protocol; author J. Postel; date September 1981; status Internet Standard.  \n   Source: [RFC Editor info for RFC 791](https://www.rfc-***/info/rfc791).",
